    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Three villages on the Hooghly became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">English East India Company</a>'s settlement from 1690, and after the Company's victory at Plassey in 1757 Calcutta was the centre of its rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>.</li><li>As the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> until 1911 it grew into the second largest city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and was the home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and of the early nationalist movement.</li><li>The famine of 1943, the communal violence of 1946 and the Partition of 1947 changed its population, and later decades brought economic decline and then recovery.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Calcutta High Court ruled in 2003 that Job Charnock, long believed to be the founder of Calcutta, was not its founder, and that the city therefore has no birthday.</p></aside>
    </section>

