    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1690</span><span class="rf-text">Job Charnock returns to Sutanuti and hoists the Company standard on the bank of the Hooghly</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1756</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siraj-ud-daulah">Siraj ud-Daulah</a> captures Calcutta and Fort William</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1757</span><span class="rf-text">The Company defeats the Nawab at Plassey, and Calcutta becomes the centre of its power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1864</span><span class="rf-text">A cyclone strikes the city and kills about 60,000 people</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1911</span><span class="rf-text">The capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> moves from Calcutta to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1943</span><span class="rf-text">Millions starve in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> famine</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1947</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partition-of-india">partition of India</a> and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> sends hundreds of thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> into the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2001</span><span class="rf-text">The city's official name changes from Calcutta to Kolkata</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kolkata lies on the eastern bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hooghly-river">Hooghly River</a> in the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> Delta, about 80 km west of the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>. Much of the city was originally wetland that was reclaimed over the decades. Excavations at Chandraketugarh, 35 km to the north, show that the region has been inhabited for over two millennia. The village of Kalikata is first mentioned in Bipradas Pipilai's Manasa Vijay of 1495, in which the merchant Chand Sadagar stops at Kalighat to worship the goddess Kali on a trading voyage, and again in Mukundaram Chakrabarti's Chandimangal of 1594 and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todar-mal">Todar Mal</a>'s taxation list of 1596.</p><p>The site of the present city held three villages: Kalikata, a fishing village; Sutanuti, a village of riverside weavers; and Gobindapur, a trading post for Indian merchants. They belonged to an estate of the Sabarna Roy Choudhury family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a>, under the Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and Mughal suzerainty. The name Kolkata comes from Kôlikata, the Bengali name of Kalikata. Proposed origins include the Bengali Kalikshetra, the "field of Kali", the nearby Kalighat, the word kilkila for a flat area, and the village's trade in quicklime and coir.</p><p>The Company's agent Job Charnock settled at Sutanuti in 1690, and the city's recorded history is dated from then. He is often called its founder, but the Calcutta High Court ruled in 2003 that the city has none.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Company completed Fort William on the east bank of the Hooghly to protect its trading factory, and after 1757 the city became the centre of its control over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. Calcutta had a population of 120,000 in 1750. Under Richard Wellesley, Governor-General from 1797 to 1805, the marshes round the city were drained, the government area was laid out along the river and its public architecture was built. The city was a centre of the Company's opium trade, in which opium bought from local traders and farmers was sold at auction in Calcutta, and much of it was then smuggled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a>.</p><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 1837 recorded 229,700 people in the city proper, of whom 3,138 were British, and a further 177,000 in the suburbs and neighbouring villages. From the early 1850s Calcutta industrialised rapidly, above all in textiles and jute, and British companies invested in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> lines and in Howrah. The Port of Kolkata was established in 1870. In the 19th century Calcutta was called the second city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. The city also became a centre of learning, with the Asiatic Society of Bengal founded in 1784, the Calcutta Medical College in 1835 and the University of Calcutta in 1857.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/siraj-ud-daulah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siraj-ud-daulah.jpg" alt="Siraj ud-Daulah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/siraj-ud-daulah">Siraj ud-Daulah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siraj_ud-Daulah.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From the 14th to the 16th century the area was under the Bengal Sultanate, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> incorporated it into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal-subah">Bengal Subah</a> in 1576. The three villages were then ruled by the Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> under Mughal suzerainty. The Mughal emperor granted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> freedom of trade in return for an annual payment of 3,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a>.</p><p>Nawab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siraj-ud-daulah">Siraj ud-Daulah</a> took the city in 1756 and renamed it Alinagar, and the Company retook it in January 1757. After Plassey in 1757 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-jafar">Mir Jafar</a> was made Nawab and the Company controlled Bengal in effect. By the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Allahabad</a> of 1765 the Company became tax collector for the Mughal emperor in Bengal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a>, while Mughal-appointed Nawabs continued to rule the province. Calcutta was declared a presidency city and had become the Company's headquarters by 1773. In 1793 the power of the Nawabs was abolished and the Company took complete control of the city and province.</p><p>Calcutta served as the de facto capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> under the Company and later the Crown until 1911, when the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a>. After independence in 1947 it became the capital of the Indian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>. From 1977 to 2011 the state was governed from Kolkata by the Left Front, led by the Communist Party of India (Marxist), and the Trinamool Congress succeeded it in 2011.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rabindranath-tagore"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rabindranath-tagore.jpg" alt="Rabindranath Tagore" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rabindranath-tagore">Rabindranath Tagore</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1926_Rabindrath_Tagore.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Georges Chevalier, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By the 1850s Calcutta had two areas: White Town, mainly British and centred on Chowringhee and Dalhousie Square, and Black Town, mainly Indian and centred on North Calcutta. Unions between Europeans and local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Muslim women were common in the 18th century and are the origin of the city's Anglo-Indian community, but by the early 19th century increasing racial intolerance made them much rarer. Alongside the British lived Bengali Hindus, who formed the majority, Marwaris, Biharis, Urdu-speaking Muslims and smaller communities of Chinese, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and others. Once home to 20,000 ethnic Chinese, the city's Chinatown fell to around 2,000 people, partly after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-indian-war">Sino-Indian War</a> of 1962.</p><p>Contact between British and Indian culture produced the babu class of urbane Indians, often bureaucrats, professionals and newspaper readers, usually from upper-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> Hindu communities, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> of the 19th century. Hickey's Bengal Gazette, printed from 29 January 1780, was the first newspaper in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The Government College of Art and Craft was founded in 1864, and Kalighat painting arose in 19th-century Kolkata. The city developed neighbourhoods with a strong sense of community, called paras, and the leisurely, freestyle conversation known as adda. In the 19th and 20th centuries writers including Bankim Chandra Chatterjee and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabindranath-tagore">Rabindranath Tagore</a> modernised Bengali literature.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1756 the Company began to strengthen the fortifications of Fort William during its rivalry with the French, which angered Nawab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siraj-ud-daulah">Siraj ud-Daulah</a>. He captured Cossimbazar and then Calcutta, which fell on 20 June 1756 after a short siege. The governor and many officials escaped down the river, leaving the rest of the garrison and the Eurasian population. Several Company officials were killed in what became known as the Black Hole of Calcutta. It is said that 123 Britons died there, but recent evidence calls the numbers into question. The Company recaptured the city on 2 January 1757. Cyclones struck in 1737 and 1864 and killed thousands, and the cyclone of 5 October 1864 killed about 60,000 people. The city and its port were bombed several times by the Japanese between 1942 and 1944.</p><p>Millions starved to death in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> famine of 1943, which came from a combination of military, administrative and natural factors. Demands for a Muslim state led in 1946 to communal violence in which over 4,000 people were killed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partition-of-india">partition of India</a> led to further clashes: many Muslims left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-bengal">East Bengal</a>, and hundreds of thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> fled into the city. In the 1960s and 1970s power shortages, strikes and the violent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naxalites">Naxalite movement</a> damaged much of its infrastructure, and in 1971 refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a> strained it further.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/subhas-chandra-bose"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/subhas-chandra-bose.jpg" alt="Subhas Chandra Bose" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/subhas-chandra-bose">Subhas Chandra Bose</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Subhas_Chandra_Bose_NRB.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown (Netaji Research Bureau, Calcutta), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Calcutta was the centre of the early nationalist movement. In 1883 it hosted the first national conference of the Indian National Association, the first avowed nationalist organisation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and the partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> in 1905 along religious lines led to mass protests. The revolutionary organisations Jugantar and Anushilan Samiti were formed there, and Bankim Chandra Chattopadhyay wrote the song <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vande-mataram">Vande Mataram</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subhas-chandra-bose">Subhas Chandra Bose</a> escaped from his Elgin Road house in Calcutta to reach <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. In 1971 the city was home to the government-in-exile of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>.</p><p>The University of Calcutta was the first modern university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>, and the Indian Museum, established in 1814, is the country's oldest museum. Among the city's imperial buildings are the Victoria Memorial and the Howrah Bridge. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabindranath-tagore">Rabindranath Tagore</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/c-v-raman">C. V. Raman</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amartya-sen">Amartya Sen</a> are among the Nobel laureates associated with it, and Mother Teresa received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> for the Missionaries of Charity, which she founded there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a> overtook it as India's most populous city in the mid-1980s, and its economy recovered after the 1990s. The official name changed from Calcutta to Kolkata in 2001. Kolkata is now the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>, with about 4.5 million people in the city proper and over 14 million in its urban agglomeration.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/amar-sonar-bangla">Amar Sonar Bangla<span>1905</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Kolkata, called Calcutta until 2001, grew from three villages on the Hooghly after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> settled there in 1690. It was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> until 1911 and a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and the nationalist movement. It is now the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>.</em></p>

